how are you feeling in terms of what  you're seeing people talking about you  know publicly versus uh kind of how you  once you synthesize all this different  data we're about to talk about how do  you feel well i personally try to remove  the emotion from it but i do feel like  on twitter or whatever you've seen a lot  of uh you know just general fear or like  anxiety and panic um in the market and  uh you know i think  this kind of near-term price action is  has  been provoking that for sure uh but you  know i'm trying to just look at this  from an objective standpoint and you  know just read whatever the data is  telling me so i don't really have any  kind of like emotional  reaction to you know what's going on i  try to just like  ignore that  got it all right so when we get started  here the first thing that uh we've got  is obviously the price uh and a lot of  that is being affected by the perp  futures dominance all the perp uh kind  of uh interest in the market etc  before we jump into the actual data  explain exactly what's going on with  these kind of futures and how that  relates to price in general what can  happen in the futures market that makes  price go up or go down as we as we look  at this data sure so anyone who follows  me on twitter has probably seen me  posting consistently for the last like  two or three weeks about open interest  um and the build up of open interest and  so what open interest is is open  interest is just telling you the amount  of contracts that are open for  for perpetual swaps  um and so the way to think of this is  these perp contracts are just  essentially bets on bitcoin's price  there's a long side and a short side  and so open interest is just telling you  how many of those contracts are open  and so it's it's kind of a way to gauge  essentially how much pressure is  building up in the perp market but it  doesn't tell you the positioning of  those  of those contracts or the you know the  market participants on both sides of the  bet so i oftentimes get asked like when  i'll post open interest can you post  the long open interest or can you post  the short open interest  and the answer is no and that's because  there's a long and short on both sides  of every single contract so at all times  the amount of longs and shorts is always  50 50 in the pert market there's never  more longs than shorts or more shorts  than longs what you're trying to do is  you're trying to understand what is the  risk that's held by both participants on  both sides of the contract so for  example  you could have like a retail d gen  that's long on one side of the contract  meanwhile on the other side of the  contract on the short side it's a market  maker and so market makers are generally  delta neutral which just a fancy term  for saying that they have no directional  bias  right so they they essentially get paid  a fee for for providing liquidity to  different exchanges and so their their  job is to essentially just provide  liquidity and  allow people to get fills for their  orders and then they get paid a fee from  the exchange for the amount of volume  that their liquidity that they're  providing to that specific exchange  um and so you know  in that example i just used right you  have the retail guy on one side and he's  just looking to you know access cheap  leverage on the other end you have a you  know market maker who has no you know  uh  directional bias because they're hedged  and that's that's where the you know  delta neutral part comes in they can  hedge either if in this case i'm saying  they're short right  you know they could hedge by going along  in the spot market and then they have no  directional bias um and so you know  that that  you know the risk profile of both  participants there is completely  different right the person taking the  risk is the retail person on the wrong  side in the example i just described  and so we can get into in a second some  of the things that  you can look at to try to gauge the  aggression and the risk that's held by  the different market participants that  are making up the open interest  but open interest is solely telling you  and by the way you can also look at the  context of the open where you know where  open interest is being opened how it's  interacting with price to also kind of  get a gauge of that  but open interest in itself just saying  that there's high open interest doesn't  mean that it's necessarily bullish or  bearish in itself it's just saying that  you know you're building up the pressure  or in  in my mind that means that you can  expect volatility right because you have  a large amount of leverage that's built  up in the system  okay so when we look at this you had a  tweet on what is this the fourth of  january and you basically were saying if  there's anything i feel confident about  in the intermediate term it's that  volatility is coming to bitcoin and you  then later followed it up with a second  tweet that uh or i'm sorry there's a  tweet before that where you basically i  don't know which direction it's going to  go so i think you were very much like  hey volatility is coming we're just kind  of uh trading sideways here and that  volatility could go up or down i don't  yet have an opinion on that all i know  is that that volatility is coming so  talk us through a little bit as to like  why were you so convinced that the  volatility was coming which ultimately  was a kind of a downward move in the  price but what was telling you that that  volatility was coming  yeah so first of all like some people  are probably gonna look like you know  listen to this who are a bit you know  less sophisticated and say well you're  just saying you know price could go up  or down like what good use is that you  know first of all like i think you you  know if you don't have an opinion you  don't always need to have an opinion and  so in this case  um i didn't think that some of the clues  from the positioning of the uh  of the of the perps was that clear to me  as to where you know this was going to  kind of resolve  um we could talk about what i think at  this current state but at the you know  the time that i put this out um and the  reasoning is because like for example  like the funding setup is just  completely different from for example  summer where you know we were talking  about a massive short squeeze because  funding was you know in a prolonged  regime of negative funding and was going  further negative as price was grinding  above the lows at the time you know the  positioning was pretty clear to me that  okay this was short um you know lately  just because we have just muted funding  i don't think it's been quite as clear  and so with that being said  it is useful when you you know when  you're expecting volatility but perhaps  don't have some kind of directional bias  because you can still  um you know make a play on that either  through options or something called move  contracts  on ftx which are essentially bets on  volatility that's why they're called  move contracts  um and so the reason i was just  expecting this was  a we had you know this massive build up  of open interest that hadn't gotten  wiped out at all  especially when you compare open  interest to market cap so this is really  important  this is the in the this section of these  four tweets the one in the bottom right  this is looking at the amount of open  interest in the pert market relative to  market cap and why that's important is  because  you need to get a gauge of how much  weight that open interest has relative  to bitcoin's market cap right because  you could have you know x amount of open  interest but if if market cap is five  times larger than what it is now then it  has a smaller overall impact on the on  the market um you know versus if  you know if the market cap was half the  size it was and we still had the same  amount of open interest then open  interest would have double the you know  effect on the market so  that ratio is a good gauge of you know  how impactful or how influenced the  market is going to be by these uh by the  derivatives  and so in combination with that the  other three charts that i have  are looking at implied volatility and so  this is the volatility that's getting  priced into options um and so the market  was pricing in really low volatility uh  we were at the lowest implied volatility  since um the end of uh summer so it was  just showing you like people were  expecting you know really low volatility  generally consolidation leads to  expansion expansion leads to volatility  um  and so like  i'm sorry expansion leads to  consolidation but um you know we've been  in this kind of period of consolidation  really tight range between 45 to 52.  meanwhile we've had this massive build  up of leverage and the options market  hasn't been pricing in that volatility  so  i was i was looking to take a bet on  volatility i you know like full  transparency i didn't execute on this um  so that's you know like what what good  is analysis if executed poorly right um  so that's kind of a lesson learned from  this for me in terms of i didn't  actually execute on that but um yeah  those are some of the things that i was  looking at and you know perhaps things  that you can apply moving forward um  between the the the dynamics in the  derivatives market as to when you can  potentially expect volatility

